{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017TBXJoNEMZrzvHPHVY5CML","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ACL Rolling Review review form overall assessment scoring scale 1-5 criteria 2025 2026\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Review Form – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform\"},{\"title\":\"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Reviewer Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"ACL ARR 2025 May | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/group?id=aclweb.org%2FACL%2FARR%2F2025%2FMay\"},{\"title\":\"CALL FOR PAPERS – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/cfp\"},{\"title\":\"🎪 ACL 2025 Reviews Are Out: To Rebut or Flee? The Harsh Reality of NLP’s \\\"Publish or Perish\\\" Circus | CSPaper Forum\",\"url\":\"https://forum.cspaper.org/topic/27/acl-2025-reviews-are-out-to-rebut-or-flee-the-harsh-reality-of-nlp-s-publish-or-perish-circus\"},{\"title\":\"How ARR works – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewing\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Area Chair Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"aclrollingreview/reviewerguidelines.md at main · acl-org/aclrollingreview\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/acl-org/aclrollingreview/blob/main/reviewerguidelines.md\"},{\"title\":\"ACL ARR 2025 July | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/group?id=aclweb.org%2FACL%2FARR%2F2025%2FJuly\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about the ACL Rolling Review assessment scoring:\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation. The excitement score is orthogonal to the assessment of the soundness of the paper.\n\nRegarding the specific 1-5 scoring scale criteria, the search results indicate that reviewers provide these three distinct scores rather than a single overall assessment on a 1-5 scale with predefined criteria. The soundness scores must be justified by the text of the review.\n\nThe search results show information about the review form and guidelines, but they don't provide detailed descriptions of the specific numerical scoring criteria (1-5 scale definitions) for the \"overall recommendation\" score or other metrics. If you need the complete scoring rubric with specific 1-5 scale definitions and criteria, you may need to access the official ACL Rolling Review form directly at the URL mentioned in the search results (aclrollingreview.org/reviewform).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2a034fb2-c99a-4077-810e-027f0ae362c6","uuid":"dc16fae5-91bc-4f70-b575-43d036839a41","timestamp":"2026-05-13T17:06:02.657Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"ACL Rolling Review review form overall assessment scoring scale 1-5 criteria 2025 2026","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01CnFidhT2bc9hW8heE8B6dP","content":[{"title":"Review Form – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"},{"title":"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/"},{"title":"ARR Reviewer Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines"},{"title":"ACL ARR 2025 May | OpenReview","url":"https://openreview.net/group?id=aclweb.org%2FACL%2FARR%2F2025%2FMay"},{"title":"CALL FOR PAPERS – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/cfp"},{"title":"🎪 ACL 2025 Reviews Are Out: To Rebut or Flee? The Harsh Reality of NLP’s \"Publish or Perish\" Circus | CSPaper Forum","url":"https://forum.cspaper.org/topic/27/acl-2025-reviews-are-out-to-rebut-or-flee-the-harsh-reality-of-nlp-s-publish-or-perish-circus"},{"title":"How ARR works – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewing"},{"title":"ARR Area Chair Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines"},{"title":"aclrollingreview/reviewerguidelines.md at main · acl-org/aclrollingreview","url":"https://github.com/acl-org/aclrollingreview/blob/main/reviewerguidelines.md"},{"title":"ACL ARR 2025 July | OpenReview","url":"https://openreview.net/group?id=aclweb.org%2FACL%2FARR%2F2025%2FJuly"}]},"Based on the search results, here's what I found about the ACL Rolling Review assessment scoring:\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation. The excitement score is orthogonal to the assessment of the soundness of the paper.\n\nRegarding the specific 1-5 scoring scale criteria, the search results indicate that reviewers provide these three distinct scores rather than a single overall assessment on a 1-5 scale with predefined criteria. The soundness scores must be justified by the text of the review.\n\nThe search results show information about the review form and guidelines, but they don't provide detailed descriptions of the specific numerical scoring criteria (1-5 scale definitions) for the \"overall recommendation\" score or other metrics. If you need the complete scoring rubric with specific 1-5 scale definitions and criteria, you may need to access the official ACL Rolling Review form directly at the URL mentioned in the search results (aclrollingreview.org/reviewform)."],"durationSeconds":4.915682031000004}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011iYUTw91BEbgpcc5UpwWDZ","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"site:aclrollingreview.org reviewform overall assessment scale soundness\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Review Form\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Reviewer Guidelines\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Area Chair Guidelines\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"Authors Guidelines\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors\"},{\"title\":\"Response to ACL 2023 Questions\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/ACL-2023-session/\"},{\"title\":\"Action Editor Form\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/actioneditorform\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Changelog\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/changelog\"},{\"title\":\"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/\"},{\"title\":\"Arr Changes December\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/ARR-changes-December/\"},{\"title\":\"CALL FOR PAPERS – ACL Rolling Review\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/cfp\"}]\n\nI found the information you were looking for. Here are the results from the ACL Rolling Review (ARR) website:\n\n## Overall Assessment Scale\n\nThe overall assessment scale includes: 5 = Consider for Award (for papers that could be considered for an outstanding paper award at an *ACL conference); 2 = Resubmit next cycle (for papers needing substantial revisions by the next ARR cycle); 1.5 = Resubmit after next cycle (for papers needing substantial revisions that cannot be completed by the next ARR cycle); and 1 = Do not resubmit (for papers that need to be fully redone or are not relevant to the *ACL community).\n\n## Soundness Score\n\nThe soundness assessment asks whether the paper is sufficiently sound and thorough, whether it clearly states scientific claims and provides adequate support for them. For experimental papers, reviewers consider the depth and breadth of research questions, technical soundness of experiments, and methodological validity of evaluation. For position papers and surveys, they consider whether the current state of the field is adequately represented. For resource papers, they consider the data collection methodology and resulting data in sufficient detail.\n\nThe soundness scale includes: 5 = Excellent (one of the most thorough studies); 4 = Strong (study provides sufficient support for all claims); and 3 = Acceptable (study provides sufficient support for its main claims).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2a034fb2-c99a-4077-810e-027f0ae362c6","uuid":"06e5d9c1-3481-4437-9f5b-cd41d3b17a2e","timestamp":"2026-05-13T17:06:04.626Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"site:aclrollingreview.org reviewform overall assessment scale soundness","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01FSznMc9JEEoireR4g39ATw","content":[{"title":"Review Form","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"},{"title":"ARR Reviewer Guidelines","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines"},{"title":"ARR Area Chair Guidelines","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines"},{"title":"Authors Guidelines","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors"},{"title":"Response to ACL 2023 Questions","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/ACL-2023-session/"},{"title":"Action Editor Form","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/actioneditorform"},{"title":"ARR Changelog","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/changelog"},{"title":"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/"},{"title":"Arr Changes December","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/ARR-changes-December/"},{"title":"CALL FOR PAPERS – ACL Rolling Review","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/cfp"}]},"I found the information you were looking for. Here are the results from the ACL Rolling Review (ARR) website:\n\n## Overall Assessment Scale\n\nThe overall assessment scale includes: 5 = Consider for Award (for papers that could be considered for an outstanding paper award at an *ACL conference); 2 = Resubmit next cycle (for papers needing substantial revisions by the next ARR cycle); 1.5 = Resubmit after next cycle (for papers needing substantial revisions that cannot be completed by the next ARR cycle); and 1 = Do not resubmit (for papers that need to be fully redone or are not relevant to the *ACL community).\n\n## Soundness Score\n\nThe soundness assessment asks whether the paper is sufficiently sound and thorough, whether it clearly states scientific claims and provides adequate support for them. For experimental papers, reviewers consider the depth and breadth of research questions, technical soundness of experiments, and methodological validity of evaluation. For position papers and surveys, they consider whether the current state of the field is adequately represented. For resource papers, they consider the data collection methodology and resulting data in sufficient detail.\n\nThe soundness scale includes: 5 = Excellent (one of the most thorough studies); 4 = Strong (study provides sufficient support for all claims); and 3 = Acceptable (study provides sufficient support for its main claims)."],"durationSeconds":6.597931887999999}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Mpdt7uoYgtc62Ns3qYX9Yx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a good picture of the ARR/EMNLP review criteria. Let me also get the full scoring details.\n\nThe ARR review form (used for EMNLP 2026 via ACL Rolling Review) has three main scores:\n- **Soundness**: 1-5 scale (5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Poor, 1=Major issues)\n- **Excitement**: 1-5 scale  \n- **Overall Assessment**: 5=Consider for Award, 4=Accept, 3.5=Lean Accept, 3=Borderline, 2.5=Lean Reject, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1.5=Resubmit after next cycle, 1=Do not resubmit\n\n\nI need to look up the specific definitions for the excitement scale and any additional scoring details.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1783,"cache_read_input_tokens":58742,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1783},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2a034fb2-c99a-4077-810e-027f0ae362c6","uuid":"cd1967fe-21af-4eb7-96ef-d95e976406bd"}
